<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Segusiavi</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gallic people of the Forez and Lyonnais on whose land Rome founded Lyon, the future capital of the Three Gauls.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Segusiavi lived between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, in the plains of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-forez">Forez</a> and the hills west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>. Caesar describes them as the first people beyond the Rhône outside the Roman Province and as clients of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Few details survive. Their territory commanded the crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> at the hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourviere">Fourvière</a>, a strategic point for trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>They joined the revolt of 52 BCE with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a>. In 43 BCE the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lugdunum</a> (Lyon) was founded on their land, and their own chief town became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feurs">Forum Segusiavorum</a> (Feurs). Their name survives in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-forez">Forez</a> region.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/segusiavi.jpg" alt="Segusiavi" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Autorité émettrice de monnaie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Argent,_fonds_celtique,_S%C3%A9gusiaves_-_btv1b11286659c_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=segusiavi" aria-label="Segusiavi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4730 509 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Segusiavi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5095 786h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5050 765h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5050 765h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day France. <a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=segusiavi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

